1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:06,390
Imagine waking up on the morning
of September 11, 2001. You get up.

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:07,950
You go to work.

3
00:00:08,420 --> 00:00:09,380
You turn on the television.

4
00:00:09,460 --> 00:00:10,910
And nothing happened.

5
00:00:11,723 --> 00:00:14,473
No planes crashed into
the World Trade Center.

6
00:00:15,323 --> 00:00:16,973
No plane hit the Pentagon.

7
00:00:17,443 --> 00:00:19,793
No plane crashed in Pennsylvania.

8
00:00:20,634 --> 00:00:22,664
New York looks completely normal.

9
00:00:23,234 --> 00:00:24,604
It is just another Tuesday.

10
00:00:25,614 --> 00:00:27,804
At first, almost nothing seems different.

11
00:00:28,670 --> 00:00:30,350
But now imagine something much harder.

12
00:00:30,430 --> 00:00:35,480
Fast-forward 25 years. It is 2026.

13
00:00:36,471 --> 00:00:39,921
You are living in a world where
September 11 never happened.

14
00:00:40,811 --> 00:00:42,421
What would that world look like?

15
00:00:43,183 --> 00:00:44,433
And more importantly.

16
00:00:44,683 --> 00:00:49,393
How much of our world today exists because
of something that happened that morning?

17
00:00:51,813 --> 00:00:53,503
Let's start with the obvious.

18
00:00:54,373 --> 00:00:58,143
There would probably be no invasion
of Afghanistan in October 2001.

19
00:00:59,548 --> 00:01:02,398
The Taliban would still
control most of the country.

20
00:01:03,248 --> 00:01:06,718
Osama bin Laden would still be
wanted by the United States.

21
00:01:07,603 --> 00:01:10,273
But the enormous military
campaign that followed

22
00:01:10,363 --> 00:01:12,993
would probably never
happen in the same form.

23
00:01:13,715 --> 00:01:16,885
Thousands of American soldiers
would never be sent there.

24
00:01:17,095 --> 00:01:20,425
And the war would have taken
a very different human toll.

25
00:01:21,173 --> 00:01:23,343
But even here, we have to be careful.

26
00:01:24,113 --> 00:01:26,463
History doesn't come with an undo button.

27
00:01:27,293 --> 00:01:30,883
The United States already had reasons
to be concerned about terrorism.

28
00:01:32,296 --> 00:01:33,726
Al-Qaeda already existed.

29
00:01:34,636 --> 00:01:36,886
The Taliban already sheltered bin Laden.

30
00:01:37,136 --> 00:01:39,946
And the Middle East was
already politically unstable.

31
00:01:40,806 --> 00:01:44,896
So removing September 11 doesn't
mean terrorism disappears.

32
00:01:45,966 --> 00:01:48,796
It means one particular
chain of events never begins.

33
00:01:49,736 --> 00:01:52,266
And that distinction
is extremely important.

34
00:01:54,996 --> 00:02:00,526
Now it gets harder. In 2003,
the United States invaded Iraq.

35
00:02:01,676 --> 00:02:05,586
Officially, the invasion was
not a response to September 11.

36
00:02:06,061 --> 00:02:08,631
Iraq was not responsible for the attacks.

37
00:02:08,721 --> 00:02:11,781
But politically, that
morning completely changed

38
00:02:11,861 --> 00:02:14,651
the environment in which
the decision was made.

39
00:02:15,418 --> 00:02:17,738
The American public had
just experienced the

40
00:02:17,818 --> 00:02:20,608
largest terrorist attack
in the country's history.

41
00:02:21,402 --> 00:02:24,472
The government was operating
under a new sense of threat.

42
00:02:24,582 --> 00:02:27,962
And the idea that another
catastrophic attack could

43
00:02:28,042 --> 00:02:30,912
happen at any moment was
no longer hypothetical.

44
00:02:31,975 --> 00:02:36,325
Without September 11, would George
Bush still have invaded Iraq?

45
00:02:37,212 --> 00:02:38,682
Maybe. Maybe not.

46
00:02:39,569 --> 00:02:42,599
There were already arguments
for regime change in Iraq.

47
00:02:43,389 --> 00:02:46,359
There were already concerns
about Saddam Hussein.

48
00:02:47,134 --> 00:02:50,904
There were already people in
Washington advocating military action.

49
00:02:51,014 --> 00:02:54,824
But the political landscape would
have been radically different.

50
00:02:55,680 --> 00:02:57,320
And that means one of
the biggest geopolitical

51
00:02:57,400 --> 00:03:04,310
events of this century becomes uncertain.
No invasion? Perhaps no occupation.

52
00:03:04,900 --> 00:03:07,850
Perhaps no insurgency in the same form.

53
00:03:08,625 --> 00:03:11,225
Perhaps no chain of events
that eventually helped

54
00:03:11,305 --> 00:03:14,715
create the environment in
which Islamic State emerged.

55
00:03:15,505 --> 00:03:17,995
But notice the wording. Perhaps.

56
00:03:18,952 --> 00:03:21,802
We are not watching an alternate
universe through a window.

57
00:03:22,532 --> 00:03:24,422
We are making an educated guess.

58
00:03:25,292 --> 00:03:27,982
And history becomes much
harder the further we go.

59
00:03:30,842 --> 00:03:33,712
So let's bring the thought
experiment closer to home.

60
00:03:34,762 --> 00:03:39,692
Imagine going to an airport in 2026,
without that morning ever having happened.

61
00:03:40,455 --> 00:03:42,745
You arrive at the terminal. You check in.

62
00:03:43,415 --> 00:03:45,445
And airport security is still annoying.

63
00:03:46,419 --> 00:03:48,109
But probably not this annoying.

64
00:03:49,161 --> 00:03:52,611
Before that morning, security
procedures were very different.

65
00:03:53,501 --> 00:03:55,601
Knives and other objects
that are now prohibited

66
00:03:55,681 --> 00:03:58,631
could sometimes be taken through security.

67
00:03:59,541 --> 00:04:01,781
Cockpit doors were not
designed around the assumption

68
00:04:01,861 --> 00:04:05,091
that passengers might try
to take over the aircraft.

69
00:04:06,144 --> 00:04:09,204
And the enormous security
infrastructure we now take

70
00:04:09,284 --> 00:04:12,694
for granted had not yet
become part of everyday life.

71
00:04:13,451 --> 00:04:17,421
The Transportation Security
Administration did not even exist.

72
00:04:18,325 --> 00:04:22,355
Today, we simply accept all
of this. Take out your laptop.

73
00:04:23,125 --> 00:04:25,695
Put your liquids into a tiny plastic bag.

74
00:04:25,965 --> 00:04:28,555
Walk through a scanner. And wait.

75
00:04:29,480 --> 00:04:32,140
And for 25 years, you took off your shoes.

76
00:04:32,220 --> 00:04:36,550
Until, in 2025, that rule quietly ended.

77
00:04:37,320 --> 00:04:38,950
Because the scanners got better.

78
00:04:39,380 --> 00:04:41,270
Not because the fear did.

79
00:04:43,940 --> 00:04:46,710
It feels normal. But it isn't inevitable.

80
00:04:47,660 --> 00:04:49,410
It is a response to history.

81
00:04:50,100 --> 00:04:52,850
And this is where the
consequences become much bigger.

82
00:04:53,663 --> 00:04:56,823
After September 11, the
United States dramatically

83
00:04:56,903 --> 00:05:00,413
expanded its ability to collect
and analyse information.

84
00:05:01,482 --> 00:05:04,212
The Patriot Act gave the
government new powers.

85
00:05:05,522 --> 00:05:08,372
Intelligence agencies
received greater resources.

86
00:05:09,202 --> 00:05:12,572
Mass surveillance became a central
issue in national security.

87
00:05:13,593 --> 00:05:16,233
And eventually, Edward
Snowden revealed just how

88
00:05:16,313 --> 00:05:18,883
extensive some of these
programmes had become.

89
00:05:19,873 --> 00:05:21,863
But here is the interesting question.

90
00:05:22,733 --> 00:05:24,723
Would mass surveillance
have happened anyway?

91
00:05:25,511 --> 00:05:26,921
Probably to some degree.

92
00:05:27,251 --> 00:05:29,641
The technology was already developing.

93
00:05:30,511 --> 00:05:32,421
The internet was growing rapidly.

94
00:05:33,531 --> 00:05:35,281
Computers were becoming more powerful.

95
00:05:36,173 --> 00:05:39,923
Governments were already interested
in communications intelligence.

96
00:05:40,873 --> 00:05:43,563
So perhaps the surveillance
state would still have grown.

97
00:05:44,463 --> 00:05:47,953
But maybe more slowly.
Maybe under different laws.

98
00:05:48,483 --> 00:05:50,193
Maybe with stronger restrictions.

99
00:05:50,543 --> 00:05:53,173
Maybe the public would
have reacted differently.

100
00:05:54,101 --> 00:05:59,131
Again, we don't know. And this uncertainty
is the real problem with our experiment.

101
00:06:02,091 --> 00:06:05,201
Because now things become
almost impossible to predict.

102
00:06:06,371 --> 00:06:08,201
Remove September 11.

103
00:06:08,871 --> 00:06:10,561
Remove the invasion of Afghanistan.

104
00:06:11,571 --> 00:06:13,501
Possibly remove the Iraq War.

105
00:06:14,268 --> 00:06:17,198
And you have changed the political
history of the Middle East.

106
00:06:17,928 --> 00:06:19,798
But that doesn't mean you get peace.

107
00:06:20,681 --> 00:06:22,151
Iran is still there.

108
00:06:22,501 --> 00:06:24,521
Saudi Arabia is still there.

109
00:06:24,601 --> 00:06:27,711
Israel and Palestine
still have their conflict.

110
00:06:28,301 --> 00:06:30,911
Syria still has its internal problems.

111
00:06:31,812 --> 00:06:34,042
Saddam Hussein is still ruling Iraq.

112
00:06:34,652 --> 00:06:37,022
And governments still
have their own interests.

113
00:06:37,932 --> 00:06:40,362
There would still be
wars. Still be terrorism.

114
00:06:40,972 --> 00:06:42,462
Still be revolutions.

115
00:06:42,712 --> 00:06:44,242
Still be political disasters.

116
00:06:45,290 --> 00:06:48,820
History doesn't become peaceful
because one disaster disappears.

117
00:06:49,670 --> 00:06:51,460
It simply becomes a different history.

118
00:06:54,730 --> 00:06:59,660
So would 2026 be better? Maybe. Maybe not.

119
00:07:00,290 --> 00:07:03,340
There are things we can
reasonably expect to be different.

120
00:07:04,090 --> 00:07:08,280
But there is no reliable way to
calculate the entire alternate world.

121
00:07:09,085 --> 00:07:12,715
And perhaps that tells us something
important about our own world.

122
00:07:13,405 --> 00:07:15,755
We often look at history
as if it was inevitable.

123
00:07:16,525 --> 00:07:20,955
As if the present was always
waiting for us. But it wasn't.

124
00:07:21,624 --> 00:07:25,644
The world we live in is the result
of billions of decisions, accidents,

125
00:07:25,724 --> 00:07:30,734
mistakes, inventions, deaths, coincidences
and completely unpredictable events.

126
00:07:31,597 --> 00:07:35,207
Change just one of them, and the
present might look completely different.

127
00:07:38,407 --> 00:07:41,537
And there is one way to think about
this that we have not used yet.

128
00:07:42,287 --> 00:07:45,327
Not through governments. Not through wars.

129
00:07:45,407 --> 00:07:46,817
Through individual lives.

130
00:07:47,702 --> 00:07:52,252
Nearly 3,000 people who died on
September 11 would still be alive.

131
00:07:53,162 --> 00:07:55,052
Their children would grow up with parents.

132
00:07:55,832 --> 00:07:58,312
Their parents would grow
old with their children.

133
00:07:59,109 --> 00:08:03,739
Some people would marry. Some would have
children. Some would start businesses.

134
00:08:03,949 --> 00:08:05,239
Some would become grandparents.

135
00:08:06,332 --> 00:08:11,342
There would be entire families in 2026
that simply do not exist in our timeline.

136
00:08:15,002 --> 00:08:18,452
And there is something almost
impossible to comprehend about that.

137
00:08:19,402 --> 00:08:21,752
A single morning didn't
just change history.

138
00:08:22,542 --> 00:08:24,652
It changed millions of private histories.

139
00:08:25,641 --> 00:08:27,331
People met different people.

140
00:08:27,621 --> 00:08:31,951
Moved to different cities. Joined
the military. Changed careers.

141
00:08:32,101 --> 00:08:33,611
Lost the people they loved.

142
00:08:34,381 --> 00:08:37,411
They made decisions they would
never otherwise have made.

143
00:08:37,801 --> 00:08:40,631
And those decisions affected other people.

144
00:08:40,721 --> 00:08:43,171
And those people affected others.

145
00:08:44,017 --> 00:08:46,787
A historical event doesn't
only create headlines.

146
00:08:47,717 --> 00:08:49,987
It creates an enormous
tree of consequences.

147
00:08:53,287 --> 00:08:55,077
So let's go back to the beginning.

148
00:08:56,067 --> 00:08:58,197
September 10, 2001.

149
00:08:59,145 --> 00:09:02,055
Imagine that day. People went to work.

150
00:09:03,025 --> 00:09:04,915
Planes flew across the United States.

151
00:09:05,585 --> 00:09:07,215
People complained about traffic.

152
00:09:08,164 --> 00:09:11,854
Someone was late for a meeting.
Someone bought a coffee.

153
00:09:12,504 --> 00:09:14,474
Someone made plans for the weekend.

154
00:09:15,464 --> 00:09:18,234
Nobody knew that history
was about to split in two.

155
00:09:18,997 --> 00:09:21,567
Except in our version
of history, it doesn't.

156
00:09:22,557 --> 00:09:25,187
September 11 arrives. Nothing happens.

157
00:09:26,092 --> 00:09:28,502
And the strange thing is, nobody notices.

158
00:09:31,692 --> 00:09:33,162
There is no breaking news.

159
00:09:33,872 --> 00:09:35,422
No emergency broadcast.

160
00:09:36,452 --> 00:09:39,582
No moment when everyone remembers
exactly where they were.

161
00:09:40,072 --> 00:09:41,462
The day simply passes.

162
00:09:42,439 --> 00:09:47,469
And 25 years later, people live in a world
shaped by events that never happened.

163
00:09:48,253 --> 00:09:51,343
They probably don't spend much
time wondering about them.

164
00:09:51,773 --> 00:09:54,403
Because you don't miss a
history you never lived.

165
00:09:55,241 --> 00:09:58,431
And maybe that is the most
unsettling thing about history.

166
00:09:59,521 --> 00:10:02,571
The world we know is not the only
world that could have existed.

167
00:10:03,446 --> 00:10:05,576
It is simply the one we happened to get.

168
00:10:06,465 --> 00:10:09,535
And somewhere in the enormous space
of all the possible histories,

169
00:10:09,845 --> 00:10:13,755
there are countless versions of our
world that were only one event away.

170
00:10:14,603 --> 00:10:19,373
Including one where September 11,
2001, was just another Tuesday.
